   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "How to give advice",
     "texto": [
      "There are several ways of giving advice with an **infinitive**:\n\n- **Tienes que** hacer más ejercicio. (obligation)\n- **Debes** comer menos azúcar. (advice)\n- **Hay que** dormir **lo suficiente**. (general)\n- **Es bueno** relajarse. **Es malo** fumar.\n- **Lo mejor es** beber agua.\n\n**¿Por qué no + present?** suggests something to a friend: **¿Por qué no vienes** al gimnasio? Here the verb is conjugated.\n\nWith **tú** you can also use commands: **come** más fruta, **bebe** agua, **duerme** más, **no fumes**.\n\nCareful: **no tienes que** correr means «you don't need to»; **no debes** fumar means «better not to»."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "Tener que, deber and hay que",
     "texto": [
      "The three take an **infinitive**, but they do not mean the same:\n\n- **tener que** + infinitive: a person's obligation. **Tienes que** descansar.\n- **deber** + infinitive: advice or duty. **Debes** comer fruta.\n- **hay que** + infinitive: general need, no subject. **Hay que** dormir.\n\nOther formulas with an infinitive: **es bueno** relajarse, **es malo** fumar, **lo mejor es** beber agua, **dejar de** fumar.\n\n**Hay que** does not change. And **debes que** is incorrect: say just **debes** + infinitive."
     ],
